Unexplained change in bowel habits, Rectal bleeding/Iron deficiency anaemia, Unintentional weight loss, Irregular friable mass on colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-013
Reason for Visit: Severe, progressive colicky abdominal pain associated with profound fatigue, recent significant weight loss, and passing dark blood in stool.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult offspring.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, escalating, crampy abdominal pain raising high clinical suspicion for a partial or impending complete large bowel obstruction in the context of significant red-flag symptoms (weight loss, rectal bleeding). Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to evaluate for surgical peritonism and determine the need for immediate emergency department transfer prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach cramps are agonizing and come in waves, I have zero energy, and I've lost two belt sizes without trying."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an insidious onset of an unexplained change in bowel habits over the last 3 months, alternating between constipation and thin, pencil-like stools. Notes passing dark melaena and occasional bright red rectal bleeding over the past 4 weeks. Today, reports severe, progressive colicky abdominal pain indicating partial bowel obstruction. Complains of tenesmus and a constant feeling of incomplete rectal emptying. Reports unintentional significant weight loss (10kg) and profound, debilitating fatigue.
Functional Status: Ambulating slowly, clutching abdomen during waves of colicky pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding potential malignancy; requires family support for transportation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg (Cachectic appearance)
General: Patient appears pale, chronically unwell, and in acute distress during episodes of colicky pain.
Physical Exam: Conjunctival pallor present, indicating probable significant anaemia. Abdominal examination reveals a distended abdomen with high-pitched, 'tinkling' bowel sounds. A palpable, firm, fixed mass is noted in the left lower quadrant. Mild generalized tenderness but no rigid peritonism. Digital rectal examination reveals dark blood on the examining finger. Clinical picture strongly supports unexplained change in bowel habits, rectal bleeding/iron deficiency anaemia, and unintentional weight loss; an irregular friable mass on colonoscopy is presumed pending urgent investigation.
Environmental Observations: Patient using heating pad on abdomen for pain relief.
Medication Reconciliation: Utilizing OTC paracetamol and ibuprofen without relief of colicky pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Colorectal Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected partial large bowel obstruction)
Differentials:

Diverticular Stricture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound weight loss, marked anaemia, and tenesmus, which are heavily indicative of malignancy rather than benign inflammatory stricture alone.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's older age of onset, palpable hard mass, and obstructive symptoms progressing over months rather than typical acute inflammatory flares with high-volume bloody diarrhoea.

Severe Fecal Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of constitutional red-flag symptoms (weight loss, melaena, significant anaemia) and the specific colicky nature of the pain pointing to mechanical narrowing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is High)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an alarming constellation of 'red flag' symptoms: profound weight loss, palpable abdominal mass, significant rectal bleeding, and clinical signs of anaemia. The acute presentation of escalating, high-pitched colicky abdominal pain strongly suggests a progressing mechanical large bowel obstruction secondary to an advancing colonic neoplasm. This high-acuity scenario demanded urgent after-hours physical examination to rule out bowel perforation or complete obstruction. Delaying care until routine hours was contraindicated given the imminent risk of closed-loop obstruction, ischemia, and perforation. Immediate referral for emergency surgical assessment and urgent cross-sectional imaging was clinically mandated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Buprenorphine 0.3mg for severe colicky pain relief. Issued an urgent referral to the Emergency Department for surgical consultation, IV hydration, and an urgent CT Abdomen/Pelvis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Stop all solid food intake immediately and consume only clear fluids. This mechanically reduces the solid bulk attempting to pass through the narrowed section of your bowel, directly preventing the severe, agonizing cramps caused by the obstruction.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission anticipated.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required. If the patient refuses transport, they must proceed to ED immediately if they begin vomiting faeculent (stool-like) material, if the pain becomes sudden and constant (rather than coming in waves), or if a high fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A definitive diagnosis requires a CT scan and ultimately a colonoscopy with biopsy.
Clinical Course: The narrowing in your bowel requires urgent hospital management, which may include surgery or a stent to relieve the blockage before further treatments are planned.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must proceed to the hospital immediately. A severe blockage in your bowel can cause the tissue to tear or die. If your pain suddenly changes from cramps to a constant, tearing agony, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A mass has grown inside your lower bowel, narrowing the passageway. Your bowel muscle is violently contracting to push material past this blockage, causing the severe waves of pain. The hospital will perform a CT scan to map the blockage and decide the safest way to relieve the pressure.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat any solid food or take any laxatives under any circumstances. Laxatives will force your bowel to contract harder against the blockage, which could cause a catastrophic rupture of the bowel wall.

